# DB ER Diagram Generator

When asked to generate an ER diagram or database relationship visualization, use the `generate.py` script in this project.

## Invocation

```bash
# From a live database
python generate.py --dsn "<connection-string>" --output er-diagram.html

# From a JSON schema file (offline)
python generate.py --schema-file schema.json --output er-diagram.html

# Specific tables only
python generate.py --dsn "<dsn>" --tables "table1,table2,table3" --output er-diagram.html
```

## Connection string formats

| Database | Format |
|----------|--------|
| MySQL | `mysql://user:pass@host:3306/dbname` |
| PostgreSQL | `postgres://user:pass@host:5432/dbname` |
| SQL Server | `sqlserver://sa:pass@host:1433/dbname` |
| SQLite | `sqlite:///path/to/file.db` |

## Key options

- `--output` — output HTML path (default: `er-diagram.html`)
- `--title` — page title shown in browser
- `--tables` — comma-separated whitelist of table names
- `--exclude` — comma-separated blacklist of table names
- `--theme` — `dark` (default) or `light`
- `--font-size` — base font size in px (default: 16)
- `--no-infer-rels` — disable automatic relationship inference

## As a Python module

```python
from generate import ERGenerator

ERGenerator(
    dsn="mysql://root:pass@localhost:3306/mydb",
    output="er.html",
    title="My System ER Diagram",
).run()
```

## Schema JSON format (offline mode)

```json
{
  "table_name": {
    "comment": "Table description",
    "cols": [
      {"key": "PRI", "col": "id",      "type": "int",     "cmt": "Primary key"},
      {"key": "MUL", "col": "user_id", "type": "int",     "cmt": "→ users.id"}
    ]
  }
}
```

Use `→ target_table.column` in the `cmt` field to explicitly annotate relationships.

## Install dependencies

```bash
pip install pymysql              # MySQL
pip install psycopg2-binary      # PostgreSQL
pip install pymssql              # SQL Server
```
